---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mm-to-inches">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="mm-to-inches" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The shop-floor conversion</h2>
    <p>
      You convert mm to inches at a bench, with a metric drawing in one hand and an imperial tape in
      the other. It is one division: <strong>divide by 25.4</strong>. The inch is defined as exactly
      25.4 mm, so 25.4 mm is exactly one inch, 12.7 mm is exactly half an inch, and 6.35 mm is exactly
      a quarter.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page also gives fractions, because you cannot find a decimal inch on a rule. It shows three
      fraction columns at once, at 1/64, 1/32 and 1/16, so you can pick the resolution your tools can
      hold.
    </p>

    <h2>Metric and imperial drill and fastener sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Metric and imperial hardware are close enough to interchange and far enough apart to ruin a
      thread. The important pairs:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Imperial</th><th scope="col">Exact mm</th><th scope="col">Nearest metric</th><th scope="col">Difference</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1/8 in</td><td>3.175 mm</td><td>3 mm</td><td>0.175 mm undersize</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3/16 in</td><td>4.7625 mm</td><td>5 mm</td><td>0.24 mm oversize</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1/4 in</td><td>6.35 mm</td><td>6.5 mm</td><td>0.15 mm oversize</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5/16 in</td><td>7.9375 mm</td><td>8 mm</td><td>0.06 mm oversize</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3/8 in</td><td>9.525 mm</td><td>9.5 mm</td><td>0.025 mm undersize</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1/2 in</td><td>12.7 mm</td><td>13 mm</td><td>0.3 mm oversize</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A 5/16 in bolt in an 8 mm hole is a clearance fit by 0.06 mm, which is fine. A 1/2 in bolt in a
      13 mm hole rattles. In the other direction, a metric bolt in an imperial hole can seize.
    </p>

    <h2>Sheet, wire and material thicknesses</h2>
    <p>
      Sheet metal and plywood are the classic trap, because the nominal name is not the measurement.
      "Half-inch" plywood is usually 12 mm (0.472 in), not 12.7 mm, and 18 mm sheet is 0.709 in, not the
      3/4 in (19.05 mm) it is sold as. Always convert the measured thickness, never the label.
    </p>
    <p>
      For fine work, one thousandth of an inch (a "thou" or "mil") is 0.0254 mm. A 0.05 mm feeler gauge
      is about two thou.
    </p>

    <h2>How the fraction snapping works</h2>
    <p>
      The converter multiplies your millimetre value by the chosen denominator, rounds to the nearest
      whole number of those units, then reduces the fraction. 20 mm is 0.7874 in. At sixty-fourths
      that is 50.4 sixty-fourths, which rounds to 50/64 and reduces to 25/32. When the snap changed the
      value, the result has an "approximate" label, so you know if a figure is exact or practical.
    </p>

    <h2>A printable reference</h2>
    <p>
      The table in the tool covers common daily values, with decimal inches next to the fraction you
      cut to. The page prints on one sheet, for the inside of a toolbox lid. It also works with the
      Wi-Fi off, because the whole conversion is in the page.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
